    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Director--Psychoeducational-Diagnostic-and-Intervention-Clinic--PDIC-_R-03012": {
      "title": "Director, Psychoeducational Diagnostic and Intervention Clinic (PDIC)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Director, Psychoeducational Diagnostic and Intervention Clinic (PDIC) Compensation: $80,000-$85,000 Department: TC Deans Office_C About this opportunity: The Psychoeducational Diagnostic and Intervention Clinic (PDIC) is a university-based training and service clinic housed within Teachers College at Ball State University. The clinic provides comprehensive psychoeducational, psychological, developmental, behavioral, and diagnostic assessment services for children, adolescents, adults, families, schools, and community agencies. The PDIC serves as a critical training site for graduate students in School Psychology and related disciplines while providing high-quality, evidence-based services to individuals throughout East Central Indiana and beyond. The clinic supports Ball State University’s commitment to community engagement, experiential learning, and interdisciplinary collaboration by offering assessment, consultation, and intervention services that address academic, behavioral, social-emotional, developmental, and learning needs. Through close partnerships with schools, healthcare providers, families, and community organizations, PDIC contributes to improved educational and behavioral outcomes while preparing future practitioners. PDIC is part of Ball State University’s Interprofessional Community Clinics (ICCs), a collaborative network of clinical and training programs that provide integrated services across disciplines. The ICCs include programs in counseling, audiology, speech-language pathology, autism services, primary care, and other health and human service professions. Through its participation in the ICCs, PDIC contributes to an interprofessional model of care that emphasizes coordinated service delivery, collaborative training, shared operational infrastructure, and improved outcomes for individuals and families across the lifespan. The Director of the Psychoeducational Diagnostic and Intervention Clinic (PDIC) is a 12-month professional staff position responsible for providing strategic, operational, and clinical leadership for the clinic. The position requires a balance of clinical leadership, student supervision, community engagement, operational oversight, and strategic planning to ensure the continued growth, effectiveness, and sustainability of the clinic. Responsibilities include teaching, supervision, clinical work, and clinic administrator oversight - and will ensure the delivery of high-quality assessment and intervention services while supporting the educational and training missions of Teachers College and the Department of Educational Psychology. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function The Director of the Psychoeducational Diagnostic and Intervention Clinic (PDIC) is responsible for providing strategic, operational, and clinical leadership for the clinic. The Director will ensure the delivery of high-quality assessment and intervention services while supporting the educational and training missions of Teachers College and the Department of Educational Psychology. The Director reports to the Associate Dean for Faculty Affairs and Strategic Initiatives in Teachers College and works closely with School Psychology core faculty to support graduate training, accreditation requirements, student supervision, and program development. The Director also collaborates regularly with the directors and leadership team of the Interprofessional Community Clinics to advance interdisciplinary service delivery, shared initiatives, and community partnerships. The position requires a balance of clinical leadership, student supervision, community engagement, operational oversight, and strategic planning to ensure the continued growth, effectiveness, and sustainability of the clinic. The clinic director’s responsibilities will include teaching, supervision, clinical work, and clinic administrator oversight. Duties / Responsibilities 1. Clinical Leadership and Service Delivery Provide leadership for the delivery of high-quality psychoeducational, psychological, behavioral, and related clinical services. Maintain clinical standards consistent with ethical, legal, accreditation, and university requirements (i.e., APTC). Conduct psychological, psychoeducational, developmental, and behavioral assessments as needed. Support the development and expansion of assessment and intervention services responsive to community needs. Ensure appropriate documentation, risk management, and quality assurance practices. Collaborator with the School Psychology EdS and PhD program in the Department of Educational Psychology. 2. Student Training and Supervision Supervise graduate student engaged in clinic-based practice, internships, and training experiences. Provide individual and group supervision in assessment, intervention, consultation, professional writing, and ethical practice. Collaborate with School Psychology faculty to support student learning outcomes, accreditation requirements, and professional competencies. Participate in the development and evaluation of clinical training experiences and student performance. 3. Clinic Administration and Operations Oversee day-to-day clinic operations, including scheduling processes, assessment materials, technology systems, record management, and workflow procedures. Collaborate with ICC administrative and operational personnel to support efficient clinic functioning. Monitor clinic utilization, financial performance, service outcomes, referral patterns, and client satisfaction. Develop and implement policies and procedures that promote operational effectiveness and service quality. Ensure compliance with HIPAA, FERPA, university policies, professional standards, and state regulations. 4. Interprofessional Community Clinic Collaboration Serve as the PDIC representative within the Interprofessional Community Clinics. Collaborate with ICC directors and leadership to support interdisciplinary service delivery and coordinated care models. Develop referral pathways and collaborative programming across clinic units. Participate in ICC planning, assessment, strategic initiatives, and quality improvement efforts. Support interprofessional education and training opportunities for students across disciplines. 5. Community Engagement and Strategic Development Cultivate and maintain partnerships with schools, healthcare providers, community agencies, and other stakeholders. Promote awareness of clinic services through outreach, presentations, and professional engagement activities. Contribute to strategic planning efforts that support clinic growth and sustainability. Support grant development, philanthropic initiatives, and external funding opportunities. Represent PDIC, Teachers College, the ICCs, and Ball State University in professional and community settings. 6. Additional Responsibilities Participate in departmental, college, and university initiatives as appropriate. Contribute to continuous improvement efforts that support student success, community engagement, and clinical excellence. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications Doctoral degree in School Psychology, Clinical Psychology, Counseling Psychology, or a related psychology discipline from an accredited institution. Licensed Psychologist in the State of Indiana as a Health Service Provider in Psychology (HSPP), or eligibility for Indiana licensure at the time of appointment. Experience conducting psychoeducational, psychological, and behavioral assessments across various populations. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with key stakeholders. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree in School Psychology from a NASP or APA-accredited program Minimum of two years of professional experience providing psychological assessment and intervention services beyond doctoral internship or postdoctoral training. Demonstrated experience supervising graduate students, trainees, or clinical staff. Indiana licensure or eligibility as a School Psychologist. Experience working within university-based training clinics. Experience collaborating with K–12 schools and educational agencies. Experience with interdisciplinary or integrated healthcare service delivery models. Experience with clinic administration, budgeting, strategic planning, quality improvement, and program development. Experience pursuing or managing grants, contracts, or external partnerships. Strong organizational, communication, and leadership skills. How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact: Shannon Dieringer ( sdieringer@bsu.edu ) Please follow upload instructions for attaching all required supporting application documents listed below. Only complete applications submitted through Workday, including the required documents, will be considered. REQUIRED APPLICATION DOCUMENTS: A cover letter addressing qualifications and interest in the position A current résumé - Please do not use the Autofill feature for resume Within the application, please provide contact information for three professional references The option to upload your transcript is available but is not required at the time of application. Original, official transcripts from the issuing institution reflecting the highest related earned degree is required at the time of hire (even if your degree is obtained at Ball State University). The application deadline is: September 28, 2026, 12:01 a.m. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer paid 24 vacation days, 12 sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. We do the same for you.  In fact, we'll cover 100 percent of the tuition costs for you to earn your undergraduate degree and 80 percent of your graduate tuition.  This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Professional and download the spreadsheet. If you are applying for a 10-month position or one with Burris or Indiana Academy, please select the corresponding calculator."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Psychologist-and-Director-of-the-Center-for-Autism-Spectrum-Disorders_R-03018": {
      "title": "Psychologist and Director of the Center for Autism Spectrum Disorders",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Psychologist and Director of the Center for Autism Spectrum Disorders Compensation: $80,000-$85,000 Department: TC Deans Office_C About this opportunity: The Center for Autism Spectrum Disorders (CASD) strives to be a comprehensive training and research hub dedicated to advancing knowledge and practice in autism spectrum disorder (ASD). CASD provides Ball State University students with hands-on training in autism diagnostic evaluation, evidence-based intervention strategies, and collaborative consultation practices. The CASD offers a wide range of services and resources, including diagnostic evaluation, family-centered support, and professional development opportunities. The core component of the CASD is to conduct innovative, interdisciplinary research that informs best practices in assessment, intervention, and service delivery for individuals with ASD across the lifespan. The mission of the CASD is threefold: (a) to provide training opportunities for undergraduate and graduate students to learn and implement autism diagnostic evaluation and intervention services; (b) to provide services and resources in partnership with the community, including intervention, consultation, and individual and group student-centered support and services; and (c) to conduct innovative research to inform practice. The CASD Director is a licensed psychologist with a PsyD or PhD in psychology or related field who has the endorsement as an HSPP and will provide leadership and oversight for the Center for Autism Spectrum Disorder, supervise students in clinical training activities, teach one course within the area of clinical expertise for the Department of Special Education, and conduct psychological evaluations for autism referrals. This is a 12-month position that can provide continuity of services throughout the calendar year. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: Provide leadership and oversight for the Center for Autism Spectrum Disorder, supervise students in clinical training activities, teach one course within the area of clinical expertise for the Department of Special Education, and conduct psychological evaluations for autism referrals. Duties / Responsibilities: 1. Program Administration Assist with oversight and coordination of clinic operations, programming, and service delivery. Contribute to outreach, community partnerships, and campus initiatives that enhance awareness and access to services. Support student training, supervision coordination, and program development initiatives for students in the department of special education, department of educational psychology, and/or closely related fields. Maintain compliance with university, ethical, and licensure standards, including documentation, confidentiality, and billing procedures. Represent the CASD, Teachers College, the ICCs, and Ball State University in professional and community settings. 2. Clinical Services & Assessment Conduct psychological assessments for community clients and university students, including clinical interviews, behavioral observations, and standardized testing. Administer, score, interpret, and communicate results of cognitive, personality, and neurodevelopmental assessments related to autism spectrum disorder (ASD) and other mental health concerns. Provide evidence-based individual and group therapy for students with ASD and co-occurring mental health conditions. Develop individualized support plans and provide psychoeducation, consultation, and family support as appropriate. 3. Interprofessional Community Clinic Collaboration Serve as the CASD representative within the Interprofessional Community Clinics. Collaborate with ICC directors and leadership to support interdisciplinary service delivery and coordinated care models. Develop referral pathways and collaborative programming across clinic units. Participate in ICC planning, assessment, strategic initiatives, and quality improvement efforts. Support interprofessional education and training opportunities for students across disciplines. 4. Teaching and Supervision Supervise graduate assistants and practicum students. Teach one class per semester in the Department of Special Education. 5. Student Support Support autistic students in navigating academic, social, and daily challenges within the university setting. Collaborate with disability services, student success programs, faculty, and community providers to coordinate care and promote inclusion for neurodiverse students. Provide consultation and training to faculty and staff regarding best practices for supporting students with ASD. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in clinical or school psychology from an accredited university. Licensed Psychologist in the State of Indiana as a Health Service Provider in Psychology (HSPP), or eligibility for Indiana licensure at the time of appointment. Expertise in the diagnosis and treatment of autism spectrum disorder. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively with key stakeholders. Strong organizational, communication, and leadership skills. Preferred Qualifications: Three years post-licensure experience. Completion of a post-doctoral fellowship in autism spectrum disorders. Experience working with autistic adults. Experience teaching undergraduate and graduate students. Board Certified Behavior Analyst Certification. Experience with interdisciplinary or integrated healthcare service delivery models. Experience with clinic administration, budgeting, strategic planning, quality improvement, and program development. Experience pursuing or managing grants, contracts, or external partnerships. How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact: Shannon Dieringer ( sdieringer@bsu.edu ) Please follow upload instructions for attaching all required supporting application documents listed below. Only complete applications submitted through Workday, including the required documents, will be considered. REQUIRED APPLICATION DOCUMENTS: A cover letter addressing qualifications and interest in the position A current résumé - Please do not use the Autofill feature for resume Within the application, please provide contact information for three professional references The option to upload your transcript is available but is not required at the time of application. Original, official transcripts from the issuing institution reflecting the highest related earned degree is required at the time of hire (even if your degree is obtained at Ball State University). The application deadline is: September 28, 2026 12:01 a.m. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer paid 24 vacation days, 12 sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. We do the same for you.  In fact, we'll cover 100 percent of the tuition costs for you to earn your undergraduate degree and 80 percent of your graduate tuition.  This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Professional and download the spreadsheet. If you are applying for a 10-month position or one with Burris or Indiana Academy, please select the corresponding calculator."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant-Lecturer-Assistant-Teaching-Professor-of-Entrepreneurship_R-03020-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor of Entrepreneurship",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 20 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor of Entrepreneurship Compensation: $60,000 - $90,000 Department: Management Start Date: 01/01/2027 About this opportunity: The Department of Management in the Miller College of Business at Ball State University welcomes applications for a full-time, 10-month contract position with a joining date of January 2027 or August 2027, based on the availability of the candidate and needs of the department. This contract position is renewable based upon the performance of the employee, teaching needs of the department, and budget. Candidates should have the ability to be physically present on Ball State campus multiple days per week and should be prepared to teach both face-to-face and online courses. Student needs will determine the final count of in-person vs online courses. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the United States without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Position Function: Primary responsibilities include teaching courses in the field of entrepreneurship and/or other areas of management (e.g., leadership, human resource management, international business, organizational behavior). Duties and Responsibilities: Teaching: Anticipated assignments include teaching four classes (12 credits) per semester in the field of entrepreneurship and/or other areas of management (e.g., leadership, human resource management, international business, organizational behavior). Service: Performing service and outreach activities for Ball State University and the community. Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree in business or related field from an accredited institution. Previous teaching experience at the undergraduate collegiate level.     Preferred Qualifications: Master’s or doctoral degree in business or related field from an AACSB-accredited institution. Significant teaching experience in entrepreneurship at an AACSB-accredited institution. Proven record of service and outreach in the field of entrepreneurship. Required Certifications/Licensures: How to apply: For inquiries about this position, please contact: Dr. Abhishek Srivastava, abhishek.srivastava@bsu.edu Please follow upload instructions and attach all required supporting application documents listed below. Only applications submitted through Workday, which include all required documents listed below, will be considered. Required Application Documents: Curriculum Vitae *Please do not use the Autofill feature for resume/vitae Letter of application Teaching Statement When prompted, please provide name and contact information for (3) references within the application Optional Documents: Transcripts may be uploaded at the time of application, however, an official transcript must be received by the issuing institution at the time of employment. Application Deadline: This position will remain open until filled with the consideration date of September 30, 2026. Candidates submitting application materials on or before this date will receive full consideration. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant-Professor-of-Marketing_R-02895-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Marketing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-25\nPosted: Posted 20 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Professor of Marketing Compensation: $132,000-$137,000 Department: Marketing Start Date: 08/01/2027 About this opportunity: The Department of Marketing in the Miller College of Business at Ball State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Marketing position beginning August 2027. We are a collaborative, student-centered, industry-connected, experientially driven, and forward-looking department committed to preparing students for successful careers in a rapidly changing marketing profession. The successful candidate will join the department at an exciting point in its development. We are strengthening our curriculum, expanding applied learning and industry engagement, and creating more intentional connections across the student experience. The candidate will contribute their own expertise and ideas while working with others to help shape what comes next. We value faculty interested in connecting classroom learning with meaningful applied and immersive experiences that help students develop the knowledge, skills, judgment, and professional readiness needed to succeed. We are seeking a candidate who is excited to contribute through excellent teaching, meaningful scholarship, collaborative engagement, and a genuine investment in the continued growth of our students, programs, and department. The ideal candidate will demonstrate a strong commitment to helping students navigate emerging developments in marketing, including evolving technologies, digital platforms, data-informed decision making, and other innovations shaping the profession. Candidates with an interest and ability to teach undergraduate and/or graduate marketing courses, particularly in one or more of the following areas are strongly encouraged to apply: digital marketing, marketing research, consumer behavior, and marketing analytics. Employer will consider sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Position Function Primary responsibilities include teaching undergraduate and/or graduate marketing courses, maintaining an active program of scholarly research, and contributing to the continued success of the Department of Marketing through meaningful service, student engagement, and collaboration with colleagues. Duties and Responsibilities Teaching: Teach 9 semester credit hours of undergraduate and/or graduate marketing courses, with assignments based on departmental needs and the faculty member's expertise. Deliver high-quality instruction in face-to-face and online environments. Develop engaging learning experiences that connect theory and practice through applied, immersive, and industry-engaged learning opportunities, when appropriate. Research: Maintain an active and productive program of scholarly research that demonstrates the potential for sustained scholarly achievement consistent with departmental, college, and AACSB expectations for tenure and promotion. Disseminate research through quality peer-reviewed publications, academic conferences, and other appropriate scholarly outlets. Contribute to a collaborative research culture through scholarly engagement, interdisciplinary collaboration, and when appropriate, the involvement and mentorship of students in research activities. Service: Contribute meaningful service to the department, college, university, profession, and broader community. Contribute to departmental initiatives that advance student success, curriculum innovation, industry engagement, and the department’s shared priorities. Collaborate with colleagues, students, alumni, and external partners to strengthen the department and enhance the student experience. Minimum Qualifications Education: Ph.D. or D.B.A. in Marketing or a closely related field from an AACSB-accredited institution. Candidates with ABD will be considered and must demonstrate satisfactory progress toward completion of the doctoral degree consistent with university policy. Experience: Evidence of, or demonstrated potential for, high-quality scholarly research. Evidence of, or demonstrated potential for, effective university-level teaching. Preferred Qualifications Evidence of effective university teaching. Teaching interests or expertise in one or more of the following areas: digital marketing, marketing research, consumer behavior, or marketing analytics. Evidence of an active research pipeline and potential for sustained scholarly productivity. Interest or experience in applied and immersive learning. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with colleagues and contribute to a collegial, student-centered department. How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact: Department Chair, Jamye Foster ( jamye.foster@bsu.edu ) Please follow upload instructions to attach all of the required supporting application documents listed below. Only complete applications submitted through Workday will be considered. REQUIRED APPLICATION DOCUMENTS: A cover letter addressing qualifications and interest in the position A current résumé or curriculum vitae - Please do not use the Autofill feature for resume Academic philosophy highlighting how your candidacy aligns with Ball State University's values Within the application, please provide contact information for three professional references The option to upload your transcript is available but is not required at the time of application. Original, official transcripts from the issuing institution reflecting the highest related earned degree is required at the time of hire (even if your degree is obtained at Ball State University). This position will remain open until filled with the consideration date: September 27, 2026. Candidates submitting application materials on or before this date will receive full consideration. Review of applications will continue until position is filled. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Biomedical-Program-Coordinator_R-02988": {
      "title": "Biomedical Program Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-24\nPosted: Posted 21 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Biomedical Program Coordinator Compensation: $16.50 - 17.84 Department: Biology Start Date: 10/12/2026 About this opportunity: The Department of Biology is one of the largest and most academically active units within the College of Sciences and Humanities. Building on a long record of excellence in teaching, research, and service, the department provides rigorous, hands-on programs that prepare students for success in the biological and biomedical sciences. Biology hosts a robust graduate program and a large biomedical sciences undergraduate program. These programs require considerable communication and support for our students. The Department of Biology at Ball State University invites applications for a full-time Program Coordinator to assist the Directors of these programs. This is a full-time, 12-month position with the potential to be hybrid. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the United States without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function The primary responsibility for this position is to provide administrative support for the Directors of the Masters, PhD, and Biomedical Sciences Programs. This includes assisting with all aspects of the programs and performing other duties as assigned by the Department Chairperson. Duties and Responsibilities: Manage Communications Respond to program inquiries and maintain student communication lists. Assist in tracking applicants through university systems (i.e. Stellic). Serve as a program communication liaison with faculty and administration. Assist with event planning Assist with organization/preparation of student orientation and faculty-student mixer events. Assist with organization and marketing of Fall Seminar Series. Assist with program management Assist with administration and tracking of TA evaluations. Assign student offices and maintain an office space inventory. Help to maintain program handbooks and websites. Participate in professional training and development as necessary. Act as back-up for the program directors. Manage applications and track student progress Manage student degree audit requests and maintain the accuracy of digital degree records, including processing exemptions and resolving record discrepancies Track applications. Assist with tracking student progress through the programs (coursework, milestones, paperwork). Perform other related duties as assigned. Maintain regular and predictable attendance. Minimum Qualifications: Associate degree in Biology, biomedical science, or an equivalent combination of education and experience. At least one year of office administrative/secretarial or related experience, Proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite including Word, Excel, Outlook, and PowerPoint. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in biology, biomedical science, or a related field from an accredited institution. Experience with online portal software systems. How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact: Tim Carter tccarter@bsu.edu Important: Please follow the upload instructions for attaching all required documents listed below. NOTE: Do not use the autofill feature for your resume. Cover Letter Resume Applications must be received before 12:01 AM on September 23, 2026. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer paid 10 to 24 vacation days, 12 sick days, and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. We do the same for you.  In fact, we'll cover 100 percent of the tuition costs for you to earn your undergraduate degree and 80 percent of your graduate tuition.  This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that show you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Staff (Exempt) and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Temporary-Classroom-Aide_R-02703": {
      "title": "Temporary Classroom Aide",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-19\nPosted: Posted 26 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPosition Title:   Temporary Classroom Aide Compensation: 14.00 per hour Department: Burris Laboratory School - Temp A Start Date: 08/10/2026 About this opportunity:   Burris Laboratory Schools welcomes applications for Temporary Classroom aide. This position is part-time, working up to 30 hours per week. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring:   Position Function : Burris Laboratory Schools welcomes applications for Temporary Classroom aide. This position is part-time, working up to 30 hours per week. Job Description : Work with K-12 students on a one-on-one basis in academic and social skills development. Provide small group assistance and instruction. Provide supervision during lunch times. Work collaboratively with administrators, faculty, parents, and students. Perform other related duties as assigned. Maintain regular and predictable attendance Minimum requirements : Must have a high school diploma or equivalent. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. Preferred Education and Experience : Associates degree in related field How to apply:   Please follow upload instructions to attach all of the required supporting documents listed below: IMPORTANT: To ensure accurate processing of your application, do not rely on a resume upload to populate the job experience section. All work history must be entered directly into the application fields. Cover Letter Resume"
