    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Title-1-Paraprofessional_R-03190-1": {
      "title": "Title 1 Paraprofessional",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPosition Title: Title 1 Paraprofessional Compensation: $15.00 - 16.26 Department: Burris Laboratory School_A Start Date: 10/01/2026 About this opportunity: Burris Laboratory School welcomes applications for a dedicated and compassionate Title I Paraprofessional. This position works up to 30 hours per week, 10 months out of the year serving Burris students by providing assistance with literacy and math strategies. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: The Title 1 Paraprofessional position is a 10-month opportunity supporting Burris Title 1 students, in accordance with the Indiana Department of Education. Contributing to a positive and professional learning environment, the Title I paraprofessional must stay current with all required certification, use literacy strategies to assess student progress toward literacy mastery and work with the Title 1 teacher, classroom teachers, and interventionists to implement and progress monitor math strategies and fact acquisition. Duties & Responsibilities: 1. Provide academic support for reading and math mastery by tutoring and assisting students individually or in small groups, reinforcing learning concepts presented by the Title 1 and/or other classroom teacher. 2. Use Tier 2 and Tier 3 literacy strategies in both individual and small groups settings, effectively communicating student needs and improvements. 3. Support classroom teacher in monitoring students during independent work to assess for and provide needed differentiation accommodations in the classroom setting. 4. Complete all required Title 1 trainings and certifications annually, as required by the Indiana Department of Education. 5. Complete and all additional training on literacy methods such as Orton Gillingham; incorporate literacy strategies in daily instruction. 6. Partner with the Title 1 and classroom teachers to design interventions and progress monitoring for individual students. 7. Recognize and respect confidentiality in all aspects of student information, understand and uphold the Federal Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA). 8 . Organizes results of student assessments and files records for the Title 1 and classroom teacher. 9. Maintain regular and predictable attendance. 10. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Associate’s degree or an additional two years of related experience will be accepted in lieu of an associate’s degree. One year experience working with students in a school setting Problem solving and conflict resolution skills   Preferred Qualifications: Experience working with students in a Title 1 setting. How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact: Sarah Gaither at sagaither@bsu.edu Please follow upload instructions for attaching all required documents listed below. Only complete applications will be considered. Required Application Documents: Resume Application Deadline: September 28, 2026, 12:01 a.m."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Instructor-of-Special-Education--K-12_R-03191": {
      "title": "Instructor of Special Education, K-12",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Instructor of Special Education, K-12 Compensation: $48,500–$74,312 Department: Burris Laboratory School Start Date: 10/12/2026 About this opportunity: Burris Laboratory School at Ball State University welcomes applications for an Instructor of Special Education. This role includes overseeing individualized programming, supporting diverse learning needs, and working collaboratively with colleagues. Responsibilities also involve maintaining ongoing communication with families and Burris partners. The ideal candidate will actively support and contribute to the fulfillment of the school’s vision and mission. Compensation/salary is determined and based upon relevant experience and highest degree earned, with a salary range of $48,500–$74,312. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Duties and Responsibilities: a. Participates as a member of a multidisciplinary team to develop, implement, and evaluate individualized education programs (IEPs) for K-12 students with identified disabilities ensuring compliance with federal, state and corporation requirements. b. Attends case conferences as Teacher of Record for students with disabilities. c. Models best-practice instructional strategies and provides actionable feedback to Ball State University preservice teachers and graduate students in Learning Center. d. Manages and assists with statewide testing programs for students with disabilities to ensure appropriate accommodations are provided and the assessments are completed in accordance with state regulations. e. Provides specialized instruction and support to students with significant academic, behavioral, communication, functional, and/or adaptive needs. f. Collaborate with general education teachers to support student participation and progress in the least restrictive environment. g. Assesses student progress toward learning targets, objectives, expectations and goals to provide feedback to students, parents, and administrators and guide future classroom instruction. h. Monitors students in a variety of educational settings (e.g. classroom, playground, hallways, restrooms, field trips, etc.) to provide a safe, secure and positive learning environment. i. Establishes and maintain appropriate relationships with students, parents, faculty and staff by communicating in a professional, courteous and confidential manner and adhering to Burris Laboratory School's social media policy. j. Demonstrates a commitment to continuous professional growth and attends and participates in professional development opportunities, faculty meetings, extra-curricular activities and committees. k. Reports incidents (e.g. suspected child abuse, suspected substance abuse, harassment, zero tolerance offenses, etc.) to administrators to maintain the personal safety of students and provide a positive learning environment that adheres to state law and Ball State University policies. l. Performs other job-related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Valid Indiana teaching license- K-12, Mild Interventions licensure. Preferred Qualifications: Valid Indiana teaching license- K-12, Intense Interventions licensure. Required Certifications: Valid Indiana Teaching License K-12 Mild Interventions Licensure (Preferred Intense Interventions licensure.) Bachelor's degree from a regionally accredited university by date of appointment; hold or demonstrate eligibility for Indiana certification in special education by date of employment. Master's degree in special education or related field of study from a regionally accredited university by date of appointment. At least three years experience teaching. How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact: Dr. Abbie Comber, aecomber@bsu.edu Please follow upload instructions and attach all required supporting application documents listed below. Only applications submitted through Workday, which include all required documents listed below, will be considered. Required Application Documents: Cover letter Curriculum Vitae *Please do not use the Autofill feature for resume/vitae Application Deadline: October 12, 2026, 12:01 a.m. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant-Teaching-Professor_R-03166-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Teaching Professor Compensation: $60,000-$65,000 Department: Physics & Astronomy Start Date: 08/16/2027 About this opportunity: An Assistant Teaching Professor of Physics faculty position is available starting August 2027. The successful application will be teaching primarily introductory courses for non-majors, though other courses may be needed as determined by the department chair and will participate in departmental service-related activities. Position eligible for yearly renewal with satisfactory progress. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Duties and Responsibilities: The successful applicant will be expected to teach full-time (12 credit hours) lecture and laboratory courses primarily in the areas of introductory physics and astronomy. The successful applicant will contribute intellectual and professional service to the department at a level associated with contract-faculty-positions. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in physics, astronomy, physics education, or related discipline from an accredited institution by date of appointment. Teaching experience at the college level, which may include typical graduate assistantship assignments. Preferred Qualifications: Experience teaching introductory physics and/or astronomy courses for majors, non-majors, and/or pre-service elementary education majors. Effective communication skills in classroom and laboratory settings. How to apply: Search Committee Chair, Dr. John Millis, jpmillis@bsu.edu Please follow upload instructions to attach all of the required supporting documents listed below: NOTE: Do not use the Autofill feature for resume: Required Documents: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Statement of Teaching Philosophy and Interest Consideration Date: to ensure full consideration, applications must be received by November 19th, 2026. (Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.) What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Temporary-Concessions-Crew-Member_R-03185": {
      "title": "Temporary Concessions Crew Member",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-08\nPosted: Posted 6 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nPosition Title:   Temporary Concessions Crew Member Compensation: $15.92/Hr Department: Recreation Services - Temp Start Date: 09/11/2026 About this opportunity:   We are excited to announce the posting of the Temporary Concession Crew Member position. This position will work a variety of athletic and special events throughout the year. Come and be a part of an exciting, fast-paced, and team-oriented environment! Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring:   Position Description: The following description is intended to indicated the kinds of duties and level of difficulty required of positions with this title and department; it is not intended to limit the right of the supervisor to assign, direct, and control the work of any employee under their supervision. Employees are expected to perform their daily work duties and responsibilities at the standards set by management. Employees are also expected to understand and apply universal precautions to their daily work duties and responsibilities. Perform a variety of tasks included but not limited to what is required in the preparation and serving of food, cashiering, cleanliness, proper inventory counts, and receiving food and beverage items and maintain a positive customer service and fan experience. Duties and Responsibilities: Accurately assemble supplies and materials; set up service area and serve food as required. Set-up, prepare, serve, and maintain proper food, non-alcoholic, and alcoholic beverage sales and handling procedures, and other duties related to the concession stand area operations. Hand wash and store pots, pants, and food service equipment and clean equipment, condiment areas, and work area, maintaining sanitation and safety. Scape and wash dishes. Assist in the maintenance and cleanliness of concession stand area. Assist in the maintenance and cleanliness of concession stand area. Disposing of materials properly. Perform checker/cashier duties using computerized machinery, terminals, calculators, and maintain records which includes, but is not limited to: Operated and maintain accurate cash drawer during shift. Count, wrap, and verify money. Keep money and reports secure at all times. Properly and legally serve alcoholic beverages in a safe and effective manner. Clean equipment and work area, maintaining sanitation and safety. Greet customers to ensure positive relationships and positive fan experience. Assist in the accurate record keeping of inventory items for both food and beverage items, cash drawer reconciliations, food temperatures, food expiration timelines within a concession stand. Set-up, operate, and clean dishwashing machinery and auxiliary equipment; move dishware and equipment to proper locations. Count and record amounts of food prepared and/or served. Grill, steam, carve, and prepare food as required. Receive, count-in, obtain and maintain supplies for storage areas and concession stand operations. Clean, prepare, store, and deliver fresh produce. Maintain a professional work environment. Wait and bus tables as assigned. Enforce all rules and regulations, including but not limited to proper payment of food and that proper procedures are following employees regarding the payment of food; report all violations immediately to management. Perform other duties as assigned. The above listed essential functions required varying degrees of independence, judgement, and discretion. All of the above listed tools, activities, functions, requirements and conditions will be used in varying degrees. Note: The duties/responsibilities of this position, and the percentage of time required to perform those duties/responsibilities may vary significantly based on shift assignment and decisions of management. All of the above duties/responsivities are to be performed at the standards set by management. Minimum Qualifications: High School diploma or equivalent. Must be 18 years or older. Must be able to read and comprehend all specified policies and procedures for safety, hazardous materials information, and University policies, procedures, and communications. No allergies to soaps, cleaning agents, oven cleaners, or other materials or environmental factors in food services, e.g., steam, dust, etc. Must have clean body, teeth, hair, nails, and clothing. Must be able to perform all physical and mental requirements outlined in each job duty/reasonability. Preferred Qualifications: Previous experience in cashiering and using a computerized cash register. Experience in fast food, snack bar, dairy bar, or similar commercial establishment. How to apply:   Please apply via Workday.    Required Documents:  Resume  Cover letter-optional but preferred  Three references should be provided    IMPORTANT: To ensure accurate processing of your application, do not rely on a resume upload to populate the job experience section. All work history must be entered directly into the application fields.      Requires successful completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Fiscal-Analyst_R-03241": {
      "title": "Fiscal Analyst",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Fiscal Analyst Compensation: $70,000-$77,000 Department: Charter Schools_A About this opportunity: The Fiscal Performance Analyst supports the oversight of Ball State University’s authorized charter schools by monitoring and analyzing financial performance, reviewing audits and budgets, and assessing fiscal stability. This role works closely with school leaders, boards, and auditors to ensure compliance with financial performance standards while providing data-driven insights to support key authorizing decisions. The position also contributes to financial reporting, dashboard development, and training to strengthen financial practices across the portfolio. This position is remote within Indiana or within reasonable proximity to Indiana. The role requires regular in-state travel to schools and periodic presence on campus; therefore, candidates must be able to travel statewide and maintain accessibility for in-person responsibilities. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the United States without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: Monitor the finances of BSU’s charter schools by analyzing all of the monthly, quarterly and annual financial statements submitted by all university authorized charter schools; analyzing the annual independent audit reports and supplement audit reports for all charter schools; evaluating those audit reports and the financial performance framework dashboards to determine whether the charter schools are meeting the financial performance indicators in our financial performance frameworks; assessing enrollment numbers to determine whether charter schools are maintaining the appropriate enrollment to remain financial stable; monitor the ratio of “soft funds” upon which the charter schools are relying in order to meet their expenditures; and ensuring that schools are filing the appropriate annual tax returns in order to maintain tax exempt status. Duties / Responsibilities: 1. Monitor the finances of BSU’s charter schools including analyzing all of the monthly, quarterly and annual financial statements submitted by all university authorized charter schools; assessing enrollment numbers to determine whether charter schools are maintaining the appropriate enrollment to remain financial stable; and monitoring the ratio of “soft funds” upon which the charter schools are relying in order to meeting their expenditures. 2. Under the leadership and direction of the Executive Director of the OCS, attend assigned schools by visiting them regularly, developing relationships with faculty and staff, and being responsive to requests within the appropriate parameters of the charter school authorizer agreement. 3. Attend budget board meetings (including all board meetings of assigned schools) to analyze financial planning performance against the OCS Financial Performance Framework and recommend interventions and oversight to the Executive Director of OCS. 4. Serve as primary financial liaison for auditors, School Board Members, CEOs and CFOs within BSU sponsored Charter Schools to ensure understanding of their financial position. Ensures compliance with university financial performance frameworks. Provides direction and oversight for reporting on financial performance frameworks and reporting. 5. Provide continual analyses of Ball State authorized charter schools’ fiscal stability. Reviews budgets, enrollment numbers, financial statements, and other supporting documents provided by charter schools to determine the fiscal stability throughout the contract term. This data is used for renewal decisions as well as for annual fiscal monitoring for each charter school and may be used in conjunction with amendment decisions, i.e., if a school wishes to expand or borrow money to build a new facility. 6. Develop, implement and maintain a fiscal dashboard to monitor and analyze the financial stability of Ball State authorized charter schools pursuant to the financial performance indicators in the Financial Performance Framework the terms of their charter agreements. Works with auditors, School Board Members, CEOs and CFOs within BSU sponsored Charter Schools to ensure understanding of the financial performance framework and dashboard and providing training associated therewith to Ball State authorized charter schools’ administrators and budget directors. 7. Collect and present qualitative and quantitative data related to the financial framework for annual performance reviews with each school's Board, as well as reviews specific to schools under renewal. 8. Participate in audit close-out meetings and conduct audit follow-up, including review of audit findings issued by the SBOA or independent auditor(s), and monitor resolution plans. 9. Develop, refine, and disseminate budget templates to Board Members, CEOs and CFOs within BSU sponsored Charter Schools to obtain monthly, quarterly and annual financial data to assess and analyze the financial stability of the charter schools, verify accuracy of data provided for use in the OCS Annual Report and submission of various IDOE reports. 10. Evaluate applications for new charter schools, including review of proposed budget and financial planning, school goals and objectives, and analyze the school/community need in the proposed location; make recommendation for approval or denial of applications. 11.Provide recommendations to Executive Director and OCS staff related to renewals, the intervention process, revocation, and contract amendments. 12. Prepare and update the Ball State University Office of Charter School budget and budget forecast. 13. Review, analyze, and update OCS financial reporting requirements for Ball State’s charter schools as required by State statute and IDOE reporting requirements to ensure the required financial data is being obtained to determine the financial stability of Ball State authorized charter schools. 14. Maintain up-to-date knowledge of Indiana school finance policy, including ADM counts, complexity index, grant funding streams (e.g., CSP, Federal funding), and fiscal implications of legislation. 15. Collaborate with OCS staff on the production of OCS statutorily required annual Charter School Accountability Report by to providing all revenue generated and expenditures incurred by OCS annually. Produce and decimate a diagnostic report assessing fiscal indicators and audit findings for each school on an annual basis. 16. Attend budget board meetings (including all board meetings of assigned schools) to analyze financial planning performance against the OCS Financial Performance Framework and recommend interventions and oversight to the Executive Director of OCS. 17. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in accounting, finance, business administration with a concentration in accounting or finance, public finance, or a closely related financial field from an accredited institution. At least three years of professional experience in education finance, public-sector finance, accounting, auditing, budgeting, or financial management, including experience reviewing and interpreting financial statements, budgets, and/or audit reports. Highly organized; strong problem solving and public relations skills. Effective oral and written communication skills; knowledge of public school finance. Proficient in Microsoft Excel and the use of spreadsheets for financial analysis, budgeting, and financial reporting. Possess and maintain a valid driver’s license and be insurable under the university’s auto fleet policy; ability to provide own transportation. Additional Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in accounting, finance, business administration, public administration, or a closely related financial field. Professional experience in K–12 education finance, charter school finance, governmental accounting, or public-sector financial management. Demonstrated experience reviewing and interpreting financial statements, budgets, independent audit reports, and audit findings. Experience assessing organizational fiscal health, financial risk, cash flow, debt obligations, and long-term financial sustainability. Experience working with auditors, CFOs/business officials, governing boards, and executive leadership and communicating complex financial information to non-financial audiences. Preferred Training / Certifications:         CPA, CGFM, CPFO, or other relevant accounting, governmental finance, or public-sector financial management credential preferred. Training in governmental accounting, school finance, auditing, internal controls, or fraud prevention preferred. How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact: Dr. Jamie Garwood, jgarwood@bsu.edu Please follow upload instructions and make certain all required documents listed below are attached before submitting. Only complete applications which include all required documents will be considered.  Required Application Documents: Cover letter Curriculum Resume/ Vitae   *Please do not use the Autofill feature for resume/vitae Application deadline: October 16, 2026, 12:01 a.m. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer paid 24 vacation days, 12 sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. We do the same for you.  In fact, we'll cover 100 percent of the tuition costs for you to earn your undergraduate degree and 80 percent of your graduate tuition.  This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Professional and download the spreadsheet. If you are applying for a 10-month position or one with Burris or Indiana Academy, please select the corresponding calculator."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Instructor-of-Counseling_R-01926": {
      "title": "Instructor of Counseling",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPosition Title:   Instructor of Counseling Compensation: 3000 Department: Counsel Psych, Social Psych & Couns - Temp Start Date: 08/17/2026 About this opportunity:   The Department of Counseling Psychology, Social Psychology and Counseling welcomes applications for part-time semester instructors who are needed for undergraduate courses for the minor in counseling or minor in peace studies, or graduate courses in counseling, counseling psychology, or social psychology. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. Please note that applications will remain active for 1 year. Therefore, if you apply for this position, you may not hear from the department until there is a need to hire an instructor. What you'll do and what you'll bring:   Duties and Responsibilities: Undergraduate course instruction for the minor in counseling, minor in peace studies or graduate courses in counseling, counseling psychology, or social psychology. Minimum Qualifications: Master’s in Psychology or related field from an accredited institution by date of appointment. Preferred Qualification: Doctoral degree in Psychology or related field from an accredited institution. How to apply:   For inquiries, please contact: Sharon Bowman-Committee Chair; sbowman@bsu.edu;   Please follow upload instructions to attach all of the required supporting documents listed below: NOTE: Do not use the Autofill feature for resume. Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae (CV)"
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Custodian_R-03226": {
      "title": "Custodian",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Custodian Compensation: $18.68 - $19.30 Department: Noyer Halls Start Date: 09/21/2026 About this opportunity: Ball State University Housing has an opportunity for a Custodian position. This opportunity comes with excellent pay and a great benefit package that includes medical and prescription plans, dental, flexible spending and health savings accounts, paid time off, holiday pay, University-funded retirement, tuition discounts, and more! This is a full-time, on-campus opportunity. This job is 7:30-4:00 PM M-F. Located at Noyer Halls Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: Under supervision, perform custodial work required for the cleaning and maintenance of facilities complying with appropriate procedures and techniques for equipment and cleaning products. Duties & Responsibilities: 1. Clean, renovate, and renew, resilient, wood, and hard floor surface finishes, i.e., dust mop, sweep, wet mop, spot mop, scrub, polish and spray buff, strip, seal, wax, etc. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, crawl, reach, balance, stand, walk, push, pull, lift, grasp, and exert up to 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of floor machines, wet/dry vacuum, scrubbers, mops, buckets, measuring cups, putty knife, cloth or sponge, eye protection, filter mask, and various chemical products, etc. Task exposes employees to inside environmental conditions, noise, chemicals, fumes, odors, dust, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 2. Maintain carpet floor coverings, i.e., vacuum, remove spots and stains, extraction cleaning of carpet and upholstered furniture, etc. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, crawl, reach, stand, walk, push, pull, lift, grasp, and exert up to 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of carpet vacuum, shampooer, wet/dry vacuum, extractor, putty knife, sponge or cloth, eye protection, and various chemical products, etc. Task exposes employees to inside environmental conditions, noise, chemicals, fumes, odors, dust, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 3. Clean and service all areas in restrooms to include, but not limited to, lavatory, toilet, urinal, showers, bathtub areas, etc. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, balance, stand, climb, walk, push, pull, lift, grasp, and exert up to 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of floor machine, wet/dry vacuum, portable vacuum, hand duster, bucket and wringer, mops, brushes, ladder, sponge, eye protection, gloves, and various chemical products, etc. Task exposes employees to inside environmental conditions, noise, chemicals, fumes, odors, dust, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions, information. 4. Dust, vacuum, clean and/or polish furniture and surfaces; clean facilities, fixtures, hardware and windows; change lighting and clean fixtures as required. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, balance, stand, climb, walk, push, pull, lift, finger, grasp, and exert up to 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of dust mop, portable vacuum, hand duster, counter brush, ladder, scaffolds, screwdriver, spray bottle, bucket, sponge, cloth, eye protection, filter mask, light bulbs and tubes, various chemical products, etc. Task exposes employees to inside working conditions, noise, chemicals, fumes, odors, dust, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 5. Collect and dispose of trash, refuse, recycling materials and debris into designated receptacles. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, balance, walk, push, lift, grasp, and exert up to 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, etc. Task requires the use of trash carts, bags and cans, dollies, gloves, etc. Task exposes employees to inside and outside environmental conditions, chemicals, fumes, odors, dust, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 6. Sweep debris, and/or sweep or shovel snow from outside entrances, landings, steps, walks, etc.; clean exterior surfaces of windows, doors, and other building fixtures. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, reach, stand, walk, lift, push, pull, grasp, and exert up to 50 pounds of force occasionally, 25 pounds of force frequently, and 10 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of brooms, snow shovel, plastic bags, squeegee, pole, rags, towels, etc. Task exposes employees to inside and outside working conditions. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 7. Clean appliances, i.e., stoves, ovens, microwaves, refrigerators, ice machines, washers, dryers, etc. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, push, pull, lift, grasp, balance, climb, stand, walk, and exert in excess of 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distances, distinguish colors, etc. Task requires the use of buckets, spray bottles, ladder, sponges, cloth, scrubbing pads, chemicals, etc. Task exposes employees to inside environmental conditions, fumes, odors, dust, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 8. Acquire and maintain knowledge of job-related chemicals, and environmental, health and physical concerns. Requires the physical ability to exert up to 10 pounds of force occasionally. Task requires the mental ability to read, comprehend and follow written instructions, procedures, precautions, etc., used in connection with custodial related work, trade publications, department handbook, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 9. Lock and unlock doors as required to perform job related duties in accordance with departmental policies and procedures. Requires the physical ability to stoop, reach, stand, walk, and exert up to 10 pounds of force occasionally. Task requires the visual ability to read written materials, inspect areas, judge distance, shape, shadows, etc. Task requires the use of keys. Task exposes employees to inside and outside environmental conditions. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 10. Change bed linens, make beds with clean linens as required. Requires the physical ability to stoop, crouch, lift, reach, stand, walk, push, pull, grasp, and exert up to 50 pounds of force occasionally, 25 pounds of force frequently, and 10 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to inspect areas, judge distances, size, shape, shadows, etc. Task requires the use of linens, carts, etc. Task exposes employee to inside environmental conditions, fumes, odors, dusts, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 11 . Arrange, move, and/or replace furniture, supplies, and equipment as required; report items requiring repair to supervisor; perform minor repairs to furniture and building fixtures. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, climb, stand, walk, lift, push, pull, grasp, and exert in excess of 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to inspect areas, judge distances, shape, shadow, etc. Task requires the use of screwdrivers, pliers, ladder, cart, etc. Task exposes employees to inside and outside environmental conditions, fumes, odors, dust, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 12. Load and unload trucks and/or other vehicles, place and store deliveries, etc., inform supervisor of needed supplies. Requires the physical ability to stoop, kneel, crouch, reach, push, pull, grasp, climb, balance, stand, walk, and exert in excess of 100 pounds of force occasionally, 50 pounds of force frequently, and 20 pounds of force constantly to move objects. Task requires the visual ability to inspect areas, judge distance, shape, shadows, etc. Task requires the use of cutting devices, ladders, carts, etc. Task exposes employees to inside and outside environmental conditions, fumes, odors, dust, gases, etc. Task requires the mental ability to receive and follow oral instructions and to read, comprehend, and follow written instructions and information. 13. Guide, assist and instruct student custodial employees. Requires all the above listed physical and mental abilities. 14. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: 1. High School diploma or equivalent (GED). 2. No allergies to soaps, cleaning agents, chemicals or environmental factors in custodial service. 3. Must be able to climb ladders, scaffolding of various heights to perform duties. 4. Must be able to read and comprehend all specified policies and procedures for safety, hazardous materials information, University policies, procedures and communications, and perform basic mathematical functions. 5. If required by assignment, you must possess and maintain a valid Driver’s License and be insurable under the university’s auto fleet policy. 6. Must be able to perform all mental and physical requirements as outlined in each job duty. 7. Must successfully pass manlift certification, if position requires it, within a reasonable period of time after hire. 8. Must have at least one year’s work experience (full time or part time). 9. You must be 18 years or older. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in custodial work. How to apply: For inquiries, please contact: Supervisor Natasha Wilson at nnwilson@bsu.edu Please follow upload instructions to attach any supporting documents.  Important: Do not use the Autofill feature when uploading your resume. Optional documents (Recommended for best consideration) 1. Resume. 2. Cover Letter detailing your qualifications for the position. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer a generous paid time off program and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust short-term disability, long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. We do the same for you.  In fact, we'll cover 100 percent of the tuition costs for you to earn your undergraduate degree and 80 percent of your graduate tuition. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow.   Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Service and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/PT-Food-Service-Cashier_R-03243": {
      "title": "PT Food Service Cashier",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-16\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPosition Title: PT Food Service Cashier Compensation: $16.94 - $16.94 Department: Tom John Food Shop Start Date: 09/21/2026 About this opportunity: Ball State University Dining has an opportunity for a PT Cashier position. Come and join the Ball State University Dining team on the beautiful Ball State campus and help create excellent customer service experiences for students, faculty, staff and employees! This position may work up to 30 hours per week. Hours: (Two week) Sunday: 4p-12:30a Monday: 5p-10:30p Tuesday: 5p-10:30p Wednesday: 5p-10:30p Thursday: 5p-10:30p Friday: Off Saturday: Off Sunday: 4p-12:30a Monday: 5p-10:30p Tuesday: 5p-10:30p Wednesday: 5p-10:30p Thursday: 5p-10:30p Friday: Off Saturday: Off Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: Under supervision, perform duties related to collection and recording of monies. Duties & Responsibilities: 1. Count, wrap, and verify money. Keep money and reports secure at all times. Requires the ability to perform basic mathematical calculations, efficiently operate a calculator, read and comprehend written materials, and be detail-oriented. 2. Open on time and maintain published meal service hours. Requires the ability to walk/stand for extended periods of time. Task requires the visual ability to direct customers to key areas. Employee is exposed to inside environmental conditions. 3. Operate electronic terminals with a high degree of accuracy in logging on, completing cash transactions, completing cash equivalency and debit account transactions, and generating a detailed daily media report at the end of each meal. A high degree of accuracy is expected. Requires the physical ability to grasp, push, pull, stoop, crouch, reach, walk, stand, lift, feel, have dexterity and strength in hands, and exert up to 20 pounds of force occasionally and 10 pounds of force frequently. Task requires the visual ability to perceive depth. 4. Prepare a manual ‘Check Out Form’ to report all monies from sales and to account for sales ‘overage’ and ‘shortage’. Form must be neatly written and completely accurate. Frequent overage or shortage is not acceptable. Requires the ability to read and comprehend written materials, perform basic mathematical calculations, grasp and handle objects, and be detail-oriented. Task requires the ability to provide written instructions and information. 5. Customer transactions must be made speedily; interact with customers with a smile, courtesy, friendliness, and helpfulness, and communicate customer concerns, questions, comments, and compliments to supervisor. Requires the ability to use computerized card reader, cash register, terminals, calculators, and scales. Task requires the mental ability to receive, comprehend, follow and provide oral instructions. 6. Maintain cash terminal, scales (where applicable), and other equipment in good condition through correct operation and cleaning procedures. Report equipment malfunctions immediately. Requires the ability to read and comprehend written materials and efficiently operate computerized machinery. Task requires the physical ability to grasp, push, pull, stoop, crouch, reach, walk, stand, lift, feel, and have dexterity and strength in hands. 7. Maintain cleanliness, neatness, attractive product display, and good organization of all areas around and including cashier station. May require any of the above-mentioned abilities . 8. Enforce all rules and regulations, including but not limited to: observation of the servery, dining room and grocery areas for possible theft such as pocketing food items or not paying; In all-you-care-to-eat locations, admit only those who have a valid meal plan or who have paid; Permit carry-out according to policy and procedures; Report all violations immediately to management. May require any of the above-mentioned abilities. 9. May be required to: wash and clean dining room conveyors, tables, chairs, service areas, other furnishings, window sills, plants, and equipment located in dining room, serve or re-supply food, snack foods, and other items, instruct dining room attendants in performance of work, assist with special meals or events, i.e., napkin folding, draping and skirting tables, placing decorations, dishing foods, setting up buffet tables, etc., and launder, dry, fold, hang, and store shirts, towels, skirting, or other dining service items as assigned. Requires the physical ability to grasp, push, pull, stoop, crouch, reach, walk, stand, lift, feel, have dexterity and strength in hands and exert up to 35 pounds of force unassisted. Task requires the ability to receive, comprehend, follow and provide oral instructions. 10. Perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: 1.Successful completion of Cashier Training Class and passing score on final test is required for positions with cashiering duties. 2.Must have clean body, teeth, hair, nails, and clothing. 3.No allergies to soaps, cleaning agents, oven cleaners, or other materials or environmental factors in food service, e.g., steam, dust, etc. 4.Must be able to read and comprehend all specified policies and procedures for safety, hazardous materials information, and University policies, procedures, and communications. 5.Must be able to communicate effectively both orally and in writing in the English language. 6.Must be able to perform all physical and mental requirements outlined in each job duty/responsibility. 7.You must be 18 years or older. Preferred Qualifications: 1.Previous experience in cashiering and using a computerized cash register. Experience in fast food, snack bar, dairy bar, or similar commercial establishment. How to apply: For inquiries, please contact: University Dining at 765-285-1967. Please follow upload instructions to attach any supporting documents.  Important: Do not use the Autofill feature when uploading your resume. Optional documents (Recommended for best consideration) 1. Resume. 2. Cover Letter detailing your qualifications for the position."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/PT-Patient-Care-Coordinator_R-02659": {
      "title": "PT Patient Care Coordinator",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nPart time\n\nPosition Title: PT Patient Care Coordinator Compensation: $15.00 - 16.26 Department: Interprofessional Community Clinics Start Date: 10/19/2026 About this opportunity: The College of Health at Ball State University invites applications for a part-time Patient Care Coordinator within the Interprofessional Community Clinics (ICCs). This position plays an important role in supporting the daily clinical operations of the ICCs while serving as a welcoming and professional point of contact for patients, clients, visitors, and community partners. The Patient Care Coordinator assists with patient registration and scheduling, clinic communications, patient record management, billing-related support activities, and front-office operations. This role is ideal for an organized, service-oriented professional who enjoys working in a collaborative healthcare environment and is committed to providing exceptional patient support and customer service. Candidates who are proactive, detail-oriented, and passionate about supporting community-based healthcare services are encouraged to apply. This position is part-time, non-exempt staff at 25 hours per week. Benefits are not available for this position. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the United States without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function:   The Part-time Patient Care Coordinator supports the clinical operations for the College of Health (CoH). The position carries out administrative duties for the Interprofessional Community Clinics (ICCs), and serves as a contact point for our patients, clients, visitors, and their family members. Duties and Responsibilities: Register and schedule patients/clients for services provided by the Interprofessional Community Clinics (ICCs). Answer phone calls, respond to emails, and field patient inquiries regarding the various service lines and associated costs for services provided by the ICCs. Assist patients with check in/out procedures for their scheduled services within the ICCs Maintain and manage patient health records including but not limited to preparing patient reports and related documents to be faxed or mailed, scanning documents for electronic patient charting, and filling patient requests for authorized disclosures of records. Ensure clinic forms and materials are available and organized which includes keeping common areas stocked with appropriate educational and promotional materials. In coordination with the ICCs' administrative team, complete financial/patient billing related tasks (e.g., complete weekly financial deposits, load insurance profiles in the electronic health records system, follow-up with accounts receivable, etc). Assist ICCs in preparing for various outreach events which may include attendance at select events throughout the year. Maintain patient confidentiality and adhere to ICCs’ policies and procedures for securing protected health information in accordance with the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA). Adhere to, and strictly follow, policies and procedures on security related to clinical spaces and patient care and safety. Perform other related duties as assigned. Maintain regular and predictable attendance. Minimum Qualifications: Eighteen months of specialized training or education beyond high school or equivalent amount of related experience. At least one year of office support experience or equivalent. Proficiency in using computer systems and software. Thorough ability to communicate with a wide variety of people in a precise and professional manner. Strong organizational skills. Ability to work autonomously and take initiative. Preferred Qualifications: Associate degree or equivalent. Previous experience in healthcare administration. Experience managing patient account balances and health insurance claims processing. How to apply: Please submit the following documents with your application: Cover letter - optional but recommended Resume - required - do not use the Autofill resume feature Please contact Blair Mattern at bsmattern@bsu.edu  with any questions regarding this opportunity. Applications must be received before 12:01AM on September 30, 2026."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant---Associate---Professor-of-Elementary-Education_R-03155": {
      "title": "Assistant / Associate / Professor of Elementary Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant / Associate / Professor of Elementary Education Compensation: $70,000 - $95,000 Department: Elementary Education Start Date: 08/02/2027 About this opportunity: Ball State University’s Department of Elementary Education invites applications for a full-time, tenure-line faculty position at the Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor rank beginning August 2027. While candidates at all ranks will be considered, we are particularly interested in applicants whose experiences and accomplishments support appointment at the Associate or Full Professor level. Salary range for Assistant Professor: $70,000 - $75,000 Salary range for Associate Professor or Professor: $75,000 - $95,000 We seek a collaborative teacher-scholar committed to preparing highly effective elementary educators and advancing knowledge in teaching and learning. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and graduate programs in elementary education, with particular emphasis on literacy, inclusive teaching practices, and curriculum and instruction. We are especially interested in candidates whose teaching, scholarship, and professional engagement reflect current research and evidence-based practices in elementary education and who can contribute leadership to the department’s teaching, research, partnerships, and program development. This position offers opportunities to teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students, supervise candidates in field-based settings, maintain a productive program of research and scholarship, collaborate with faculty and school/community partners, and contribute to the ongoing development of innovative educator preparation programs. Candidates at the Associate or Full Professor level should demonstrate a sustained record of scholarly productivity and professional contributions appropriate to rank. Employer will consider sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Duties and responsibilities :   Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in elementary education, with particular emphasis on literacy, inclusive teaching practices, and curriculum and instruction; supervise practicum and other field-based experiences; establish and maintain an active program of research and scholarly publication; pursue opportunities for external funding as appropriate; engage in partnerships with schools, families, communities, and other educational organizations; advise and mentor graduate students, including doctoral students; collaborate with faculty on program development and improvement; provide leadership appropriate to experience and rank; and provide professional university, college, and departmental service. Salary range for Assistant Professor: $70,000 - $75,000 Salary range for Associate Professor or Professor: $75,000 - $95,000      Minimum Qualifications:    Earned doctorate in Elementary Education, Literacy, Curriculum and Instruction, or closely related field from an accredited institution by the date of appointment.  At least three years (6 academic semesters/24 months) of teaching experience in an elementary (K-6) setting  Demonstrated record of scholarly productivity, as evidenced by refereed presentations at regional, national, or international conferences and/or scholarly publications such as refereed journal articles, book chapters, or books.  For appointment at the Associate or Full Professor level, a record of teaching, scholarship, and service commensurate with expectations for the respective rank.  Preferred Qualifications:    University-level teaching experience (at least one academic semester/3 months).  Expertise literacy instruction and knowledge of current research and evidence-based approaches to literacy teaching, including developments associated with the Science of Reading.   Knowledge of, and experience with, using current approaches to inclusive teaching, culturally responsive and sustaining pedagogies, and/or equitable educational practice while teaching learners with a wide range of strengths, needs, experiences, and backgrounds  Experience supervising preservice teachers or other educator candidates in field-based settings.  Demonstrated commitment to and experience with partnerships involving schools, families, communities, and/or community agencies.  Record of seeking and/or obtaining external grant funding.  Experience effectively integrating educational technologies into teaching, including online or technology-enhanced instruction.  Evidence of effective collaboration with colleagues and other educational partners.  For candidates at the Associate or Full Professor level, evidence of leadership in one or more areas such as program development, curriculum innovation, graduate education, research initiatives, externally funded projects, professional organizations, or school and community partnerships.  Experience mentoring graduate students, doctoral students, and/or early-career faculty  A sustained record of scholarly productivity and professional engagement appropriate to rank.   Required License/Certifications: How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact: ELED Department Chairperson, Jackie Sydnor jtsydnor@bsu.edu   Important: Please follow upload instructions for attaching all required documents listed below. Only complete applications will be considered. REQUIRED APPLICATION DOCUMENTS: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Statement of Teaching Philosophy Within the application, you will be asked to provide names and contact information for three (3) professional references. This position will remain open until filled with a consideration date of October 19, 2026, 12:01 a.m. Candidates submitting application materials on or before this date will receive full consideration. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant-Professor-of-Elementary-Education_R-03168-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Elementary Education",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Professor of Elementary Education Compensation: $70,000 - $75,000 Department: Elementary Education Start Date: 08/02/2027 About this opportunity: Ball State University’s Department of Elementary Education welcomes applications for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor available August 2027. We seek a collaborative teacher-scholar committed to preparing highly effective elementary educators and advancing knowledge in teaching and learning. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and graduate programs in elementary education, with particular emphasis on literacy, inclusive teaching practices, and curriculum and instruction. We are especially interested in candidates whose teaching, scholarship, and professional engagement reflect current research and evidence-based practices in elementary education and who are committed to meaningful partnerships with schools and communities. This position offers opportunities to teach and mentor undergraduate and graduate students, supervise candidates in field-based settings, develop a productive program of research and scholarship, collaborate with faculty and school/community partners, and contribute to the ongoing development of innovative educator preparation programs. Employer will consider sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Duties and responsibilities :  Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in elementary education, with particular emphasis on literacy, inclusive teaching practices, and curriculum and instruction; supervise practicum and other field-based experiences; establish and maintain an active program of research and scholarly publication; pursue opportunities for external funding as appropriate; engage partnerships with schools, families, communities, and other educational organizations; advise and mentor graduate students, including doctoral students; collaborate with faculty on program development and improvement; and provide professional university, college, and departmental service.  Minimum Qualifications: Earned doctorate in Elementary Education, Literacy, Curriculum and Instruction, or closely related field from an accredited institution by the date of appointment.  At least three years (6 academic semesters/24 months) of teaching experience in an elementary (K-6) setting.  Demonstrated potential for or record of scholarly productivity, as evidenced by refereed presentations at regional, national, or international conferences and/or scholarly publications such as refereed journal articles, book chapters, or books.  Preferred Qualifications: University-level teaching experience (at least one academic semester/3 months).  Expertise in literacy instruction and knowledge of current research and evidence-based approaches to literacy teaching, including developments associated with the Science of Reading.   Knowledge of, and experience with, using current approaches to inclusive teaching, culturally responsive and sustaining pedagogies, and/or equitable educational practice while teaching learners with a wide range of strengths, needs, experiences, and backgrounds  Experience supervising pre-service teachers or other educator candidates in field-based settings.  Demonstrated commitment to and experience with partnerships involving schools, families, communities, and/or community agencies.  Record of seeking and/or obtaining external grant funding.  Experience effectively integrating educational technologies into teaching, including online or technology-enhanced instruction.  Evidence of effective collaboration with colleagues and other educational partners.  Required Certifications: How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact: Elementary Education Department Chairperson, Jackie Sydnor jtsydnor@bsu.edu   Important: Please follow upload instructions for attaching all required documents listed below. Only complete applications will be considered. REQUIRED APPLICATION DOCUMENTS Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Statement of Teaching Philosophy References: Within the application, you will be prompted to provide the names and contact information for three (3) professional references. This position will remain open until filled with a consideration date of October 19, 2026, 12:01 a.m. Candidates submitting application materials on or before this date will receive full consideration. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Producer-Director_R-03179-1": {
      "title": "Producer Director",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-17\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Producer Director Compensation: $55,000 - $60,000 Department: University Media Services About this opportunity: The Producer/Director position plays a vital role in university support from University Media Services. The P/D acts as project manager for the larger productions that UMS puts together, shoots footage, edits, and oversees all aspects of that project. The P/D also manages the Production Assistants that are working on the project, as well as students. This position provides critical oversight and ensures deadlines are met, production standards are adhered to, and supporting staff and students are provided an opportunity to learn and add to their skill sets. The P/D also supports Ball State Public Media and is responsible for producing content for their use. This position supports the ESPN initiative when students and other staff are unavailable, and produces and/or directs live events such as Commencement, Nurse Pinning ceremonies, Welcome Week events, Homecoming student competitions, and other university events. P/Ds also coordinate with faculty to provide dynamic elements to coursework. P/Ds are experienced professionals who are able to mentor students in advanced production techniques, while giving constructive criticism and feedback, real-world insight, and advice to help round out the students’ education. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S without sponsorship. This role requires an on-campus presence; it is not a remote position. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: Plan, design, produce, and direct instructional, promotional, broadcast and corporate programs for University Media Services. Duties & Responsibilities 1. Serve as project manager that is responsible for preliminary project assessment, creation of a project’s financial and resource budgets, and creation of a project team in conjunction with the Production Manager. 2. Follow up on production tasks and report the project’s status and progress through the utilization of computer software that tracks resources, financial commitments, and billing statistics of UMS production projects. 3. Consult with UMS associates and other production staff, university faculty, staff and administrators, and corporate clients to design and implement media programs from concept to completion, which includes developing budgets (finances and resources), creating project timelines, communicating and coordinating project activities, directing or supporting on-location and studio crews, traveling when necessary, conducting computer based post production duties such as editing and sound mixing which incorporate digital effects and digital audio, maintaining a comprehensive log of all work related to specifically assigned projects, and performing post project evaluations. 4. Act as Director and/or Producer for live and live-to-tape events, on location and in-studio, delivered to broadcast or streaming platforms. 5. Coordinate the recording of footage on location and in-studio, as camera operator, lighting director, audio director, and/or other functions to meet the needs of the project. 6. Meet with corporate clients while working on projects through UMS Corporate Services to establish working budgets and to establish professional working relationships with those clients. 7. Identify and learn new and emerging technologies to enhance the production of direct instructional, promotional, broadcast, and corporate programs for University Media Services. 8. Develop, maintain, and assist colleagues with technical knowledge in an environment marked by constantly and rapidly changing technology. 9. Supervise students, the number of which is dependent upon the individual project; supervise other Producer/Directors and production staff dependent upon the scope of the project. 10. Serve as a mentor for UMS projects, including adherence to approved project outlines, budgets, purchases, production schedules, and final approvals of finished project; monitor progress on a weekly or bi-weekly schedule; seek approval for changes, additions, or revisions to project; set-up criteria to monitor status, approve final scripts, and be present during asset acquisition and post-production; follow all university policies for the recommendation and approval for all purchases, travel, and associated paperwork for the Production Manager; serve as the mentor and producer for the assigned projects with the quality of these projects being reviewed and judged by the Production Manager. 11. Provide production leadership for all projects; coordinate and schedule facilities, staff, and volunteers; oversee the documentation and display of items to be sold, sponsor boards, and keys. 12. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution. At least two years of experience in audio/video production, computerized editing, camera operations, script writing, electronic graphics, and lighting. Must possess and maintain a valid driver's license and be insurable under the university's auto fleet policy. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in telecommunications or related field. Experience in non-linear editing, such as Adobe Premiere Pro or Final Cut Pro; three years of experience in computerized editing, camera operations, script writing, electronic graphics, and lighting.   How to apply: For inquiries, please contact: Keith Huffman at the following email address:  khuffman@bsu.edu Please list three professional references within the online application. Important:  Do not use the Autofill feature when uploading your resume. Please follow the upload instructions to attach all required supporting documents listed below. Required Documents: Cover Letter Resume What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer paid 24 vacation days, 12 sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. We do the same for you.  In fact, we'll cover 100 percent of the tuition costs for you to earn your undergraduate degree and 80 percent of your graduate tuition.  This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Professional and download the spreadsheet. If you are applying for a 10-month position or one with Burris or Indiana Academy, please select the corresponding calculator."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Prism-Project-assistant_R-03294-1": {
      "title": "Prism Project assistant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPosition Title:   Prism Project assistant Compensation: hourly $15 Department: School of Music - Temp Start Date: 09/18/2026 About this opportunity:   Work with Prism Project 2026 Qualifying candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship What you'll do and what you'll bring:   This position will coordinate scheduling of the spring program. Assist with recruiting of Ball State volunteers. Reach out to community outlets to recruit community members. Assist with website production. How to apply:   Apply via Ball State University Workday. Required Documents: Resume"
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Instructors-of-Journalism-and-Strategic-Communication_R-03338": {
      "title": "Instructors of Journalism and Strategic Communication",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nPart time\n\nPosition Title:   Instructors of Journalism and Strategic Communication Compensation: $3,000 per three-credit hour class Department: School of Journalism & Stratgc Comm - Temp Start Date: 01/11/2027 About this opportunity:   The School of Journalism and Strategic Communication is a supportive community of faculty, staff, and students driven to pursue knowledge and share truth that spurs progress and adds value to our world. Our faculty are expert researchers and practitioners in journalism and strategic communication dedicated to outfitting students with the expertise, confidence, and connections to lead our fields. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you'll bring:   Position Function: The School of Journalism and Strategic Communication invites applications for part-time instructors to teach online and/or on-campus, undergraduate and/or graduate courses beginning in January 2027 in advertising, journalism, media analytics, AI, public relations, sports public relations, design and/or photo journalism.     Duties and Responsibilities: Teach online and/or on-campus, undergraduate and/or graduate courses beginning in Spring 2027 in advertising, journalism, media analytics, AI, public relations, sports public relations, design and/or photojournalism.  Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree from an accredited college or university in advertising, journalism, public relations, sports administration, mass communications, or field related to the classes taught by the date of appointment. OR A Bachelor’s Degree and evidence demonstrating a high level of professional proficiency and expertise in advertising, journalism, public relations, sports public relations, mass communications, or field related to the classes taught by the date of appointment. Evidence may include: (1) high-quality portfolio and/or résumé including 5+ years of experience in relation to courses to be taught;(2) evidence of leadership/management experience and/or evidence of professional experience in highly competitive professional media environments;(3) nationally recognized license or certification;(4) significant professional awards; OR(5) high-quality peer-reviewed scholarly and/or juried creative activities.         Preferred Qualifications:           Terminal degree from an accredited college or university in advertising, journalism, public relations, mass communications, or related field relevant to the classes taught; five years teaching experience; and five years professional media experience. How to apply:   Dr. Johnny Sparks, Director and Professor-Committee Chair; jvsparks@bsu.edu Please follow upload instructions to attach all of the required supporting documents listed below: IMPORTANT: To ensure accurate processing of your application, do not rely on a resume upload to populate the job experience section. All work history must be entered directly into the application fields. Three references will need to be provided. Required Documents: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae (CV)"
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant-Professor-of-Information-Systems-and-Operations-Management_R-03186": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Information Systems and Operations Management",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Professor of Information Systems and Operations Management Compensation: $130,000-$135,000 Department: Info Systems & Operations Mgt (Qiannong Start Date: 08/02/2027 About this opportunity: The Department of Information Systems and Operations Management in the Miller College of Business at Ball State University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning August 2027. Candidates with an interest and ability to teach undergraduate and/or graduate business analytics and information systems courses and conduct quality research in the same fields are encouraged to apply. Employer will consider sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Duties and Responsibilities: Responsibilities: teaching nine semester hours each fall and spring semester in subjects such as but not limited to: business analytics, Information Systems, Networking, Database, cyber security, AI in business; assignment may include undergraduate and graduate courses, which may be delivered on-line, synchronously or asynchronously. Additional activities will include scholarly activity and service. ​ Minimum Qualifications: Earned doctorate in Business Analytics and Information Systems or closely related fields from an accredited institution. Candidates with ABD will be considered and must demonstrate satisfactory progress toward completion of the doctoral degree consistent with university policy. Evidence of, or demonstrated potential for high-quality scholarly research and effective university-level teaching. Preferred Qualifications: Record of scholarly publications in peer-reviewed quality journals; experience in creative teaching methods such as experiential-learning, service-learning, immersive-learning, or other instructional innovations; and on-line teaching; evidence of active involvement in external grant writing; business experience in the field of Information Systems or related areas; ability to teach and conduct research in Business Analytics and Information Systems. How to apply: For inquiries about this opportunity, please contact: Department Chair, Dr. Qiannong Gu, qgu@bsu.edu Important: Please follow upload instructions for attaching all required documents listed below. Only complete applications will be considered. REQUIRED APPLICATION DOCUMENTS Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae (Do not use the Autofill resume feature.) References: Within the application, you will be prompted to provide the names and contact information for three (3) professional references. Optional Documents: Teaching Evaluations Teaching Philosophy Applications must be received before 12:01 AM on October 28, 2026. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant-Lecturer-Assistant-Teaching-Professor-of-Mathematical-Sciences_R-03281": {
      "title": "Assistant Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor of Mathematical Sciences",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Lecturer/Assistant Teaching Professor of Mathematical Sciences Compensation: $52,000 - $62,000 Department: Mathematical Sciences Start Date: 08/16/2027 About this opportunity: The Department of Mathematical Sciences is seeking applicants for a full-time non-tenure line faculty position as Assistant Lecturer or Assistant Teaching Professor of Mathematical Sciences (Statistics/Data Science specialization) beginning August 16, 2027. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Duties & Responsibilities:   Teach approximately 12 credits per term of courses in statistics, data science, data analytics, core curriculum courses in quantitative reasoning, and other courses such as precalculus depending on Department need and applicant qualifications. Pursue a program of professional development and perform service activities for the Department.  Evidence of high-quality teaching and significant service are necessary to be eligible for reappointment.   Minimum Qualifications:   Master’s degree in statistics, data science, mathematics, or closely related field from an accredited institution by date of appointment, with at least 18 graduate semester hours of coursework in statistics, data science, or mathematics. Teaching experience at the college level. Effective written and oral communication skills; commitment to excellence in teaching as demonstrated by a teaching statement and/or teaching evaluations.   Preferred Qualifications:  Doctorate degree in statistics, data science, mathematics, or closely related field by date of appointment. Experience teaching advanced statistics or data science classes is highly desirable, as is experience with programming languages such as R, SAS and/or Python.  Experience or interest in online instruction. How to apply: For inquiries, please contact: Dr. Ahmed Mohammed, Search Committee Chair, amohammed@bsu.edu Please follow upload instructions to attach all of the required supporting documents listed below.  Important:  Do not use the autofill feature for resume. Required documents: Cover letter addressing the stated responsibilities and qualifications Teaching statement that outlines teaching philosophy and experience Curriculum Vitae Master’s transcript Optional documents: Doctoral transcript if applicable List of courses taught Additional materials supporting excellence in teaching such as teaching evaluations Original transcripts showing your highest degree will be required upon hire.  Consideration Date: to ensure full consideration, applications must be received by November 6th, 2026. (Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date, until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice to applicants.) What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet."
